from common import *
S=[
section('8.1','Sets, elements and subsets','Sets and subsets',
 'Define a set clearly, list each element once and distinguish “element of” from “subset of”.',
 ['A set is a well-defined collection. x [[math:\\in]] A means x is an element of A; B [[math:\\subseteq]] A means every element of B is also in A. Order and repeated listings do not change a set.',
  'The empty set [[math:\\emptyset]] has no elements and is a subset of every set. A set with n distinct elements has 2ⁿ subsets, because each element is either included or excluded.'],
 ex('Subsets of {a,b,c}','Choose included or excluded for each element.', ['2×2×2 = 8 subsets.','[[math:\\emptyset]], {a}, {b}, {c}, {a,b}, {a,c}, {b,c}, {a,b,c}.'], '8 subsets.'),
 '[[math:\\in]] relates an element to a set; [[math:\\subseteq]] relates one set to another. {a} [[math:\\subseteq]] {a,b}, but a [[math:\\in]] {a,b}.',[
 q('8.1.1','fluency','List the elements of A = {even numbers from 1 to 10}.','{2, 4, 6, 8, 10}.','Apply the membership rule once to each candidate.',v('[n for n in range(1,11) if n%2==0]',[2,4,6,8,10])),
 q('8.1.2','fluency','How many subsets has a 3-element set? A 4-element set?','8 and 16.','Use 2ⁿ.',v('[2**3,2**4]',[8,16])),
 q('8.1.3','reasoning','Are {1,2,2,3} and {3,2,1} the same set? Explain.','Yes. Repetition and order do not matter; both contain exactly 1, 2 and 3.','A set records membership, not listing order.',v('set([1,2,2,3])==set([3,2,1])',True)),
 q('8.1.4','reasoning','For A={a,b}, decide: a[[math:\\in]]A, {a}[[math:\\subseteq]]A, [[math:\\emptyset]][[math:\\subseteq]]A, {a}[[math:\\in]]A.','True, true, true, false (unless {a} itself were listed as an element).','Distinguish elements from subsets.',v('[True,True,True,False]',[True,True,True,False])),
 q('8.1.5','application','A club chooses any subset of 5 activities, including none. How many choices?','32.','2⁵.',v('2**5',32)),
 q('8.1.6','challenge','How many subsets of {1,2,3,4,5} contain 1?','16.','Fix 1 as included; choose freely among the other four.',v('2**4',16))],{'kind':'subset_tree','elements':['a','b','c'],'caption':'Each element has two choices: in or out. Three elements give 2³ = 8 subsets.'}),
section('8.2','Venn diagrams and set operations','Venn operations',
 'Place the overlap first, then the “only” regions, then the outside region.',
 ['Union A [[math:\\cup]] B contains elements in A or B or both. Intersection A [[math:\\cap]] B contains elements in both. Complement A′ contains elements in the universal set [[math:\\xi]] that are not in A.',
  'For finite sets, n(A[[math:\\cup]]B)=n(A)+n(B)−n(A[[math:\\cap]]B). Subtract the overlap once because it was counted in both totals.'],
 ex('Complete a Venn diagram','[[math:\\xi]] has 30 students; 17 help at a fair, 14 at a concert, 6 at both.', ['Both: 6. Fair only: 17−6=11. Concert only: 14−6=8.','At least one: 11+6+8=25. Neither: 30−25=5.'], 'Regions 11, 6, 8, 5.'),
 '“A or B” in mathematics includes both unless the question says “exactly one”.',[
 q('8.2.1','fluency','A={1,2,3,4}, B={3,4,5}. Find A[[math:\\cup]]B and A[[math:\\cap]]B.','{1,2,3,4,5} and {3,4}.','Union collects all; intersection keeps shared elements.',v('[sorted(set([1,2,3,4])|set([3,4,5])),sorted(set([1,2,3,4])&set([3,4,5]))]',[[1,2,3,4,5],[3,4]])),
 q('8.2.2','fluency','In a 30-student group, 17 like F, 14 like C and 6 like both. Find all four Venn regions.','F only 11; both 6; C only 8; neither 5.','Subtract the overlap from each total; subtract the union from 30.',v('[17-6,6,14-6,30-(17+14-6)]',[11,6,8,5])),
 q('8.2.3','reasoning','Why is n(A[[math:\\cup]]B) not always n(A)+n(B)?','The intersection is counted twice, so subtract it once.','Use inclusion–exclusion.',v('17+14-6',25)),
 q('8.2.4','application','Of 40 people, 22 take tea, 18 take coffee and 7 take both. How many take neither?','7.','Union 22+18−7=33; 40−33.',v('40-(22+18-7)',7)),
 q('8.2.5','reasoning','If A[[math:\\subseteq]]B, what are A[[math:\\cap]]B and A[[math:\\cup]]B?','A and B.','Every element of A is already in B.',v('[sorted(set([1,2])&set([1,2,3])),sorted(set([1,2])|set([1,2,3]))]',[[1,2],[1,2,3]])),
 q('8.2.6','challenge','n(A)=12, n(B)=9 and n(A[[math:\\cup]]B)=17. Find n(A[[math:\\cap]]B).','4.','12+9−17.',v('12+9-17',4))],{'kind':'venn','universe':30,'left':'Fair','right':'Concert','regions':[11,6,8,5],'caption':'Overlap first: 11 + 6 + 8 + 5 = 30.'}),
section('8.3','Number sets and the real line','Number families',
 'Classify a number by the sets it belongs to, not by the way it is written.',
 ['Natural numbers [[math:\\mathbb{N}]] are counting numbers (here 1,2,3,…); whole numbers add 0; integers [[math:\\mathbb{Z}]] add negatives; rationals [[math:\\mathbb{Q}]] are ratios of integers with nonzero denominator. Real numbers [[math:\\mathbb{R}]] include rationals and irrationals.',
  'Thus natural numbers are a subset of whole numbers, which are a subset of integers, which are a subset of rationals, which are a subset of reals. A terminating or repeating decimal is rational. Non-repeating, non-terminating decimals such as √2 and π are irrational.'],
 ex('Classify −3, 0, 0.75 and √2','Use the most specific familiar set, then note wider membership.', ['−3 is an integer and rational.','0 is whole, integer and rational.','0.75 = 3/4 is rational.','√2 is irrational and real.'], 'All four are real; only √2 is irrational.'),
 'A number can belong to several sets. “Rational” does not mean “fraction-looking”; 0.5 and 1/2 are both rational.',[
 q('8.3.1','fluency','Classify −4, 0, 5 and 1/2 as natural, whole, integer, rational or real (give the most specific listed).','−4 integer; 0 whole; 5 natural; 1/2 rational. All are real.','Use the nesting of number sets.',v('[number_family(x) for x in [-4,0,5,0.5]]',['integer','whole','natural','rational'])),
 q('8.3.2','fluency','Which are irrational: √2, π, 0.25, 1/3?','√2 and π.','0.25=1/4 and 1/3 are ratios of integers.',v('[is_irrational_symbol(s) for s in [\'sqrt2\',\'pi\',\'0.25\',\'1/3\']]',[True,True,False,False])),
 q('8.3.3','reasoning','Is every integer rational? Is every rational an integer?','Yes; no. For example −3=−3/1 is rational, but 1/2 is not an integer.','Integers are a subset of rationals.',v('F(-3,1).denominator==1 and F(1,2).denominator!=1',True)),
 q('8.3.4','application','Place −2, 0, 1/2 and 2 on a number line in order.','−2 < 0 < 1/2 < 2.','Negative values lie left of zero.',v('sorted([-2,0,0.5,2])',[-2,0,0.5,2])),
 q('8.3.5','reasoning','Explain why 0.333… is rational.','It equals 1/3, a ratio of integers.','Repeating decimals are rational.',v('F(1,3)',1/3)),
 q('8.3.6','challenge','Find a rational and an irrational number between 1 and 2.','Sample: 3/2 and √2.','1.5 lies between; √2≈1.414 also lies between.',v('[1<F(3,2)<2,1<sqrt(2)<2]',[True,True]))],{'kind':'number_sets','caption':'Natural numbers sit inside whole numbers, integers, rationals and reals. Irrationals are real but not rational.'}),
section('8.4','Sets in surveys and decisions','Survey counts',
 'Define the universal set and categories before counting; state whether “or” includes overlap.',
 ['A survey count is only meaningful with its universal set. “Pool users” may include people who also use the gym; “pool only” excludes them.',
  'Use a Venn diagram or table to organise counts, check that regions sum to the total, and translate the result into a decision with a stated assumption.'],
 ex('A centre audit','80 members: 44 pool, 37 gym, 18 both.', ['Pool only 26; gym only 19; both 18; neither 17.','At least one: 63, which is 78.75% of 80.'], 'Regions 26, 18, 19, 17; 78.75% use at least one.'),
 'A percentage needs its denominator. 37/80 and 19/80 answer different questions about gym use.',[
 q('8.4.1','application','80 members: 44 pool, 37 gym, 18 both. Find the four regions.','26, 18, 19, 17.','Subtract overlap, then subtract union from 80.',v('[44-18,18,37-18,80-(44+37-18)]',[26,18,19,17])),
 q('8.4.2','application','What percentage use at least one facility?','78.75%.','63/80×100.',v('(44+37-18)/80*100',78.75)),
 q('8.4.3','reasoning','Is “more than a third use the gym” true? Discuss both meanings.','Yes for all gym users: 37/80=46.25%. No for gym only: 19/80=23.75%.','The category definition changes the claim.',v('[37/80*100,19/80*100]',[46.25,23.75])),
 q('8.4.4','application','Pool-only pass $4, gym-only $3, combined $6. Find income if each member buys the relevant pass.','$26×4 + 19×3 + 18×6 = $269.','Use the exactly-one and both regions.',v('26*4+19*3+18*6',269)),
 q('8.4.5','reasoning','Why should a survey report state the universal set?','The same count can mean different proportions in different populations.','Percentages and “neither” depend on the total and the denominator.',v('63/80*100',78.75,contains=['denominator'])),
 q('8.4.6','challenge','50 people: 34 like vanilla, 29 chocolate, 7 neither. Find both and vanilla only.','Both 20; vanilla only 14.','Union 43; overlap 34+29−43.',v('[34+29-(50-7),34-(34+29-(50-7))]',[20,14]))])
]
